10315.text:
Looking at these coordinates (‑25.0, ‑69.0), the location lies in the high Andean pre‑cordillera of Chile's Antofagasta Region (II Región), east of the Cordillera de Domeyko and just south‑southeast of the Salar de Punta Negra (~2,900 m at 24.6°S, 69.2°W), and west‑northwest of the high Andean salares such as Lagunas Bravas (~4,100 m at ~25.1°S, 68.7°W). This is land (Atacama/Puna desert terrain of gravel plains, salar margins and volcanic hills), not open water or a lake, and it is inland, well above sea level.

Interpolating between the salar floor to the northwest (~2,900 m) and the rising Andean plateau to the southeast (~4,100 m) over this ~50‑km spacing places the surface roughly 3,500 m above EGM2008 mean sea level.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 3500}

6782.text:
Looking at this coordinate pair (15.0°N, 65.0°E), this lies in the Arabian Sea — specifically in the deep Arabian (Abyssal) Basin, southwest of the Indus Fan and south of the Murray Ridge, on oceanic crust of the Indian Plate. There is no land or grounded ice at this location; it is open ocean over deep abyssal plain.

The abyssal plain in this part of the Arabian Basin typically has seabed depths on the order of ~3,800–4,000 m below mean sea level (EGM2008). As the question requires seabed (not sea surface) elevation with a negative sign for underwater terrain, the appropriate value is approximately −3,800 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3800}

10829.text:
Looking at the coordinates: 31°S, 121°W places this point in the South Pacific Ocean, roughly 950 km west of the East Pacific Rise and thousands of kilometers from the nearest land (South America ~4800 km to the east, Easter Island ~1300 km to the NE). This is unambiguously open ocean, so the surface type is Water and the value must be the seafloor depth relative to EGM2008 (a negative number).

Estimating the bathymetry: the point sits on the Pacific Plate a moderate distance from the Pacific–Nazca spreading center. With a full spreading rate of ~140–150 mm/yr for the East Pacific Rise at this latitude, the crustal age at ~950 km from the ridge is on the order of 12–14 Ma. Applying the standard age–depth relation (d ≈ 2500 + 350√t, t in Ma) gives about 3700 m, but this part of the Southeast Pacific lies within the broad Pacific superswell/abyssal-hill province, where the observed seafloor is several hundred meters deeper than the simple theoretical curve — typical abyssal-plain depths here are 4300–4700 m. A value near 4500 m below sea level is the most reasonable estimate.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4500}

2297.text:
Looking at 65.0°N, 95.0°E: this is inland central Siberia (Krasnoyarsk Krai, Evenkiysky District), well east of the Yenisey, on the Central Siberian Plateau in the upper Bolshaya Bakhta / Syverma Plateau region. There is no ocean or large standing water at this exact coordinate, so the surface is land — **Solid**.

For elevation: the Yenisey at this latitude sits near ~25 m, about 235 km to the west, and the terrain rises onto the dissected plateau of the Syverma Plateau region. Plateau surfaces here run roughly 500–600 m, while the upper Bakhta valley floor (which passes near this latitude/longitude) cuts down to roughly 350–450 m. A middle estimate for this point is approximately 450 m above EGM2008 mean sea level.

