14082.text:
The point 67.0°S, 95.0°W lies well offshore in the Southern Ocean (Bellingshausen/Amundsen Sea sector), northwest of Peter I Island and roughly 600+ km north of the Eights Coast/Abbott Ice Shelf grounding line. This is deep abyssal seafloor north of the continental slope — open ocean, no land, ice shelf, or grounded ice at this position.

Thus the surface is Water, and the reported value is the seabed elevation (negative, deep abyssal plain/slope base of the Bellingshausen basin region, on the order of 4–5 km below sea level).

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4500}

13374.text:
Looking at the coordinates: 59.0°S, 71.0°W places this point in the Southern Ocean — northwest of the Antarctic Peninsula, in the deep Bellingshausen Sea / southwestern Drake Passage region. This is far from the Peninsula's continental shelf break (roughly 64–67°S along this longitude) and far west of the Shackleton Fracture Zone (~58–60°W), so it is open abyssal seafloor of the Bellingshausen Plain/adjacent abyssal region. No land, grounded ice, or floating ice shelf occurs here; only the ocean and its bed.

Sediment-covered old oceanic crust in this sector typically sits around 4 km below sea level (roughly 3,500–4,500 m for the Bellingshausen abyssal plains), so a representative bed elevation is about −4,000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4000}

10247.text:
Looking at the coordinates: 23.0°S, 155.0°E places this point in the open Coral Sea, well east of the Queensland continental slope (which lies near 153°E at this latitude) and south of the southernmost Coral Sea reefs (Frederick Reefs ~21.0°S 154.4°E, Kenn Reefs ~21.2°S 155.8°E), and west of the Lord Howe Rise / Chesterfield Plateau (which lies near 158–161°E here).

This is deep oceanic crust of the Coral Sea Basin — the abyssal plain floor, at typical depths of roughly 4.5–5.0 km. Since the point is ocean, the relevant elevation is the seabed (negative), not any sea surface.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4800}

7319.text:
Looking at the coordinates 9.0°N, 59.0°E, this point lies in the open Arabian Sea (northwestern Indian Ocean), well over 500 km from the nearest land (Socotra/Somaliland/Oman). It sits in the deep ocean basin north of the Carlsberg Ridge (the Arabia–India spreading centre, whose axis runs roughly E–W near 8–8.5°N in this longitude range) and east of the Owen Fracture Zone (near 57.5°E at this latitude).

So the surface is ocean water — the value to report is the seabed elevation (negative, below EGM2008 mean sea level). Roughly 50–100 km north of the slow-spreading ridge axis, crustal subsidence plus the ridge's rugged relief and thin distal Indus Fan sediment puts the seafloor at abyssal depths of approximately 3.5–4.5 km; a mid-range value for this location is about 4.2 km below sea level.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4200}

4251.text:
Looking at 43.0°N, 43.0°E, this falls in the Greater Caucasus main ridge region of Svaneti (Georgia), roughly 3 km south of Tetnuldi (4858 m, ~43.025°N/42.995°E) and about 9 km west‑southwest of Shkhara (5193 m, ~43.01°N/43.105°E), in the high glaciated headwaters of the upper Enguri/Adishi system. This is unequivocally land (with grounded ice in the accumulation zone), not water.

Interpolating the ridge crest (≈43.02°N at 43.0°E) down the south flank toward the upper Enguri glacier tongue (~2650–2800 m a few km south), the point sits on high glaciated/rocky terrain of the accumulation zone, at approximately 3900 m above EGM2008 sea level.

