870.text:
Looking at the coordinates 81.0°N, 121.0°E, this lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Siberian (Laptev Sea) margin, far offshore (nearest land: the Lena Delta and Severnaya Zemlya, several hundred kilometres away). The point sits beyond the Laptev Sea shelf edge (which runs near ~78°N in this sector) and near the base of the Eurasian continental slope, on the southeastern flank/abyssal side of the Nansen Basin, just southwest of the eastern termination of the Gakkel Ridge (~81.5–82°N, 128°E). Thus it is open ocean (sea ice is ignored, and any ice here is floating seasonal ice), so the type is "Water" and the elevation to report is the seabed depth.

The shelf break (200 m) lies roughly 330 km to the south; with the typical Laptev margin slope gradient (~1:90–1:100), the seafloor at 81°N is near the base of slope, in the deep Nansen Basin sector, i.e. about 3.5–3.8 km below sea level.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3600}

5565.text:
The coordinates 29.0°N, 151.0°E place the point in the open Northwest Pacific, well east of the Izu–Bonin Trench (≈142.5°E) and south of the Shatsky Rise (≈32–38°N, 158–165°E), and west of the Marcus–Wake seamount trend. This is old, deep Pacific Plate crust (Jurassic–Cretaceous age) in the abyssal plain region of the Mariana/Northwest Pacific Basin — no land, no shelf, no grounded ice.

Normal plate depth for crust of that age is ~5,600–5,900 m, with the outer-rise bulge having decayed by this distance from the trench. So the surface is water (ocean), and the elevation reported is the seabed depth as a negative value, ≈ −5,800 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5800}

3595.text:
Looking at the coordinates: 51.0°N, 171.0°E places the point in the northern Pacific Ocean, southwest of the Near Islands (Attu at ~52.9°N, 173.2°E; Agattu at ~52.4°N, 173.7°E), i.e. south of the western end of the Aleutian arc and on the Pacific Plate side of the western Aleutian Trench. It lies far from any land (the westernmost Aleutian land is ~250 km to the northeast; the Commander Islands are much farther west/north), and it is not over any exposed seamount (the Emperor Seamount chain axis is at ~170.2°E here, in the documented Meiji–Ojin gap, so the seafloor is abyssal plain/outer-rise terrain).

Therefore the surface is ocean water, and the required value is the seabed elevation (a large negative number). The region is old Cretaceous Pacific crust with substantial sediment fill, adjacent to the Aleutian Trench's western termination, giving abyssal depths on the order of 5.5 km below sea level.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5500}
